#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125449} :activity/id #uuid "15348d0c-df15-5fed-9671-f77adb0de3d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050642 :topic/id #uuid "5ba25c95-b937-5921-95e0-7207aa7b83f4" :topic/math-academy-id 1875} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125802 :tutorial/content "The domain of a radical function is limited to the set of $x$-values that make the expression under the square root positive or zero.\n\nFor example, the domain of $\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nor $x \\in [0,\\infty)$ in interval notation.\n\nOn the other hand, the domain of $\\sqrt{-x}$ is given by\n\n$$\n-x \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nx \\leq 0\n$$\n\nThus, the domain of $\\sqrt{-x}$ is $x \\in (-\\infty, 0]$.\n\nAs another example, the domain of $\\sqrt{x-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nx -1 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nor $x \\in [1, \\infty)$ in interval notation.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x-1}\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cbae5f645d7774e114c483df27c8493c7e6a7f4fcf807ac7a14c54e4afa62105.png)\n\nIn general, to find the domain of a radical function of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{g(x)}\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\ng(x) \\geq 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "149ca930-55b2-5261-97a0-91c331aecf92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3797 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "12034629-2e9d-5e9e-9428-382566508681" :step/math-academy-id 21142 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124548 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124357 :question/id #uuid "1a085da3-d8eb-52eb-94ea-4969fb74a83f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5876"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f00311f-e61b-5845-b19b-59cfbdb5871a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049799}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125151}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "00a42fa0-7cac-5722-a8c6-a9c77cbe1379" :step/math-academy-id 21143 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125451}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125803 :tutorial/content "Now, let's examine the range of a transformed radical function. For example, what is the range of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -3\\sqrt{5x-2}+7\n$$\n\nThe range of $\\sqrt x$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x-2} \\ge 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then adding $7$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5x - 2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5x - 2} & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5x - 2} + 7 & \\le 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,7]$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/2651dbfeff426df9973c56c60e1d947a069b5b8c7a12281b095a878ba3de0f42.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "709c3ae9-1f28-596c-acf1-bd755cb5f318" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3798 :tutorial/title "The Range of a Transformed Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "ddc439de-a04d-570e-a224-ae11bc6f93b3" :step/math-academy-id 21144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124549 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124358 :question/id #uuid "bf9e3a92-47fa-5969-8d05-2d28114f3036" :question/math-academy-id "e-5877"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc785eb1-cb7e-5368-a092-51f87b7f7c35" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125147}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Transformed Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "3976c517-061c-59fb-bf37-f3bd18026474" :step/math-academy-id 21145 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125511}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124550 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124359 :question/id #uuid "cdb0a166-a880-5efb-a747-ac8f940ff50b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5878"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5e23379-0f5d-5691-b10f-5125325ffa70" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125149}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformed Square Root Function Given Some Properties"} :step/id #uuid "b5da1008-3709-56f8-9b9d-9e24db297a01" :step/math-academy-id 21146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124581 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124389 :question/id #uuid "fa7bd088-d496-5862-80a0-cd20083b2754" :question/math-academy-id "e-7944"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58b93ad7-d548-58cb-9cc5-0bcd79e28e4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125234}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "90d4f739-6ff8-5669-b57b-b2a88263ccc9" :step/math-academy-id 29636 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125827 :tutorial/content "To find the roots of a transformed radical function, we must set the function expression equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFor instance, what are the roots of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt{2x+1} + 5\n$$\n\nIn this case, we set\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x+1} + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 1} + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2x + 1} &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots." :tutorial/id #uuid "a0803789-82d9-5ffd-91db-c1d02974a064" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4810 :tutorial/title "The Roots of a Transformed Square Root Function"} :step/id #uuid "df14bf6d-23b8-5ed1-9e7b-0662c3ed3d6f" :step/math-academy-id 29854 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125507}}] :activity/title "Properties of Transformed Square Root Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124687}]}
